$answerbox[3]</p>

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Normal Distribution: Critical Z-values and invNorm areas ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
// IMathAS does not allow custom function definitions in the control block.
// We must use a pre-computed lookup table for common critical values 
// or a mathematical approximation written inline.

// Part 1: Right-tail area
// We pick from a set of values where we know the z-score to avoid unallowed macros.
$options1 = array(
  array("area" => 0.01, "z" => 2.326),
  array("area" => 0.02, "z" => 2.054),
  array("area" => 0.05, "z" => 1.645),
  array("area" => 0.10, "z" => 1.282),
  array("area" => 0.15, "z" => 1.036)
);
$pick1 = randfrom(0, 1, 2, 3, 4);
$rightArea = $options1[$pick1]["area"];
$z1 = $options1[$pick1]["z"];
$leftArea1 = 1 - $rightArea;

// Part 2: Middle percentage
$options2 = array(
  array("pct" => 0.90, "z" => 1.645),
  array("pct" => 0.95, "z" => 1.960),
  array("pct" => 0.99, "z" => 2.576)
);
$pick2 = randfrom(0, 1, 2);
$midPct = $options2[$pick2]["pct"];
$z2abs = $options2[$pick2]["z"];
$tailArea = (1 - $midPct) / 2;

$anstypes = "choices,numfunc,numfunc";

// Part 1: Which area to use for invNorm
$questions[0] = array(
  "The area to the right, `$rightArea`",
  "The area to the left, `" . round($leftArea1, 3) . "`",
  "The area in the middle, `" . (1 - 2*$rightArea) . "`",
  "The total area, `1`"
);
$answer[0] = 1; 

// Part 1: The critical z
$answer[1] = $z1;

// Part 2: The z-score for the middle percent
$answer[2] = $z2abs;

$strflags[0] = "ignore_case,trim_whitespace";
$strflags[1] = "ignore_case,trim_whitespace";
$strflags[2] = "ignore_case,trim_whitespace";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>Consider the standard normal distribution (mean `\mu = 0` and standard deviation `\sigma = 1`).</p>

<p><b>Part (a):</b> You want to find the critical value `z` such that the area to the <i>right</i> of `z` is `$rightArea`.</p>
<p>Since the <code>invNorm</code> function on most calculators and software computes the area to the <i>left</i>, which area should you input into <code>invNorm</code> to find this value of `z`?</p>
<p>$answerbox[0]</p>

<p>Using that area, find the critical value `z`. Round your answer to three decimal places.</p>
<p>`z =` $answerbox[1]</p>

<hr />

<p><b>Part (b):</b> Find the positive z-score that bounds the middle `$midPct` percent of the standard normal curve (the value `z` such that the area between `-z` and `z` is `$midPct`). Round your answer to three decimal places.</p>
<p>`z =` $answerbox[2]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = 'Part (a): The <code>invNorm</code> function always calculates the area from the left tail up to the z-score. If the area to the right is ' . $rightArea . ', the area to the left is ' . round($leftArea1, 3) . ' (calculated as ' . '1 - ' . $rightArea . '). Using <code>invNorm(' . round($leftArea1, 3) . ', 0, 1)</code>, we find z = ' . $z1 . '.';
$solutionguide .= "\n\nPart (b): To find the middle " . ($midPct * 100) . "% of the curve, we leave " . round((1 - $midPct) * 100, 2) . "% in the two tails combined. This means each tail contains " . round($tailArea * 100, 2) . "% of the area. The positive z-score is the boundary for the right tail, which is the same as the <code>invNorm</code> of the area to the left of the right boundary: ' . round(1 - $tailArea, 3) . '. This gives z = ' . $z2abs . '.';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Sampling Distributions - Sum Interpretation Grading Checklist ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
// Scenario: Sum of weights of a sample of 5 bags of flour.
// Population mean weight: 5.0 lbs, Population std dev: 0.2 lbs.
// Sample size n = 5.
$n = 5;
$popMean = 5.0;
$popStd = 0.2;

