<li><strong>R4 Interpretation.</strong> The probability, whatever its value, is read as the long-run fraction of mornings with `$n` orders whose total is above `$cutoff`.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Response A.</strong> Mean of `T`: `$n($muTxt) = $meanT`. Standard deviation of `T`: `$sigTxt sqrt($n) = $sdT`. The counts per order are strongly right-skewed, but `$n` orders is a large sample, so by the Central Limit Theorem `T` is approximately normal. Then `z = ($cutoff - $meanT)/$sdT = $z`, so `P(T > $cutoff) = $pProb`. Over many mornings of `$n` orders, about $pPct percent of them would total more than `$cutoff` items.</p>

<p><strong>Response B.</strong> Mean of `T`: `$n($muTxt) = $meanT`. Standard deviation of `T`: `$sigTxt sqrt($n) = $sdT`. The totals from different mornings will follow a normal curve, since there are many possible totals and the middling ones are the most common. Then `z = ($cutoff - $meanT)/$sdT = $z`, so `P(T > $cutoff) = $pProb`. Over many mornings of `$n` orders, about $pPct percent of them would total more than `$cutoff` items.</p>

<p><strong>Response C.</strong> Mean of `T`: `$n($muTxt) = $meanT`. Standard deviation of `T`: `$sigTxt/sqrt($n) = $seMean`. Then `z = ($cutoff - $meanT)/$seMean = $zC`, so `P(T > $cutoff)` is essentially 0. Over many mornings of `$n` orders, almost none of them would total more than `$cutoff` items.</p>

<p><strong>Response D.</strong> Mean of `T`: `$n($muTxt) = $meanT`. The standard deviation is `$sigTxt`, so `z = ($cutoff - $meanT)/$sigTxt = $zD`. The value `$zD` is far out in the tail, so the probability is essentially 0.</p>

<p><strong>Part a.</strong> Which rubric criteria does Response A satisfy? Check every one that applies. $answerbox[0]</p>

<p><strong>Part b.</strong> Which rubric criteria does Response B satisfy? Check every one that applies. $answerbox[1]</p>

<p><strong>Part c.</strong> Which rubric criteria does Response C satisfy? Check every one that applies. $answerbox[2]</p>

<p><strong>Part d.</strong> Which rubric criteria does Response D satisfy? Check every one that applies. $answerbox[3]</p>

<p><strong>Part e.</strong> Now total the checklist. Which grade does each response earn? $answerbox[4]</p>

