$solutionguide = "<p><strong>Step 1. Work the problem correctly.</strong> The $n orders are placed independently, so the total has mean $n times $muTxt = $meanT items and standard deviation $sigTxt times the square root of $n = $sdT items. Individual orders are strongly right-skewed, but $n is a large sample, so the Central Limit Theorem makes the total approximately normal. With `z = ($cutoff - $meanT)/$sdT = $z`, the probability that the total exceeds $cutoff is about $pProb.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2. Grade each response against the four criteria.</strong> Response A earns all four points: correct center $meanT, correct spread $sdT, a Central Limit Theorem justification, and a long-run reading of the probability. Response B has the center and the spread right and reads the probability correctly, but its reason for normality, that there are many possible totals, is not the Central Limit Theorem and never uses the size of the sample, so it loses R3 and earns 3. Response C has the center right and does read the probability as a long-run fraction, but it uses the standard error of the mean, $sigTxt divided by the square root of $n, instead of $sigTxt times the square root of $n; that shrinks the spread to $seMean and pushes the probability to essentially 0. It also gives no justification for normality, so it earns 2. Response D states the center correctly but uses the standard deviation of a single order, $sigTxt, instead of the standard deviation of the total, offers no justification for normality, and never reads the probability as a long-run fraction, so it earns 1.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3. Total the points.</strong> A earns 4, B earns 3, C earns 2, D earns 1.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A gets 4, B gets 3, C gets 2, D gets 1. In part a mark all four criteria, in part b mark R1, R2 and R4, in part c mark R1 and R4, and in part d mark R1 only.</p>";

