$solutionguide = "<p><b>Part a.</b> Each of the $n people shakes hands with $nMinus1 other people, so counting person by person gives $nPairs greetings. But each handshake has been counted twice, once by each of the two people involved, so the number of handshakes is $nPairs divided by 2, which is $H.</p>";
$solutionguide .= "<p><b>Part b.</b> Here the total number of handshakes is known and the number of people is not. If there are k people, then k times k minus 1, divided by 2, must equal $H2. The positive value that works is k = $m. Check: $m times $mMinus1 is $mPairs, and half of that is $H2.</p>";
$solutionguide .= "<p><b>Part c.</b> A handshake is exactly an unordered pair of people: the order of the two does not matter, and nobody is paired with himself or herself. A 2-person committee is an unordered pair of people, and a game between two teams in a round-robin is an unordered pair of teams, so both of those are counted by the same expression. A president and a vice-president form an ordered pair, which gives n times n minus 1 rather than half of it. The diagonals of a polygon with n vertices come to n times n minus 1, divided by 2, minus n, once the n sides are removed. Seating two people in two different chairs is also ordered, giving n times n minus 1 again.</p>";

