<tr><td>Rosa: number of shifts</td><td>$r0</td><td>$r1</td><td>$r2</td><td>$r3</td><td>$r4</td><td>$r5</td><td>$r6</td><td>$Nr</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Camila claims she is the more punctual one. They agree to settle the argument by comparing the share of shifts on which the delivery was more than 3 minutes late. In every part, enter the share as a percent, written as a number without a percent sign.</p>

<p><strong>a.</strong> What percent of the Camila table is more than 3 minutes late? $answerbox[0]</p>

<p><strong>b.</strong> What percent of the Rosa table is more than 3 minutes late? $answerbox[1]</p>

<p><strong>c.</strong> Now move the boundary down so that 3 is inside it. What percent of each table is 3 or more minutes late?</p>

<p>Camila: $answerbox[2]</p>

<p>Rosa: $answerbox[3]</p>

<p><strong>d.</strong> The two shares in parts a and b came out equal, but the two shares in part c did not. Which statement best explains this? $answerbox[4]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1 &mdash; The two totals are not the same.</strong> Camila recorded ' . $Nc . ' shifts and Rosa recorded ' . $Nr . ' shifts, so the two tables do not rest on the same number of observations. Comparing counts between them would be unfair; only shares are comparable.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 2 &mdash; Count what sits above the boundary.</strong> More than 3 minutes means 4, 5 or 6 minutes. The Camila table has ' . $c4 . ' + ' . $c5 . ' + ' . $c6 . ' = ' . $aboveC . ' such shifts. The Rosa table has ' . $r4 . ' + ' . $r5 . ' + ' . $r6 . ' = ' . $aboveR . ' such shifts. As shares that is ' . $aboveC . ' out of ' . $Nc . ' = ' . $pctC . ' percent, and ' . $aboveR . ' out of ' . $Nr . ' = ' . $pctR . ' percent. The two shares agree exactly, so a boundary drawn just above 3 does not separate the two tables at all.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 3 &mdash; Move the boundary onto 3.</strong> Counting 3 or more minutes folds the value at exactly 3 back in. Camila has ' . $c3 . ' + ' . $aboveC . ' = ' . $lowC . ' such shifts, which is ' . $pctC3 . ' percent. Rosa has ' . $r3 . ' + ' . $aboveR . ' = ' . $lowR . ' such shifts, which is ' . $pctR3 . ' percent. The two tables are now far apart. Not a single count changed between the two questions; only the position of the boundary changed.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Step 4 &mdash; Why the boundary decides it.</strong> The value 3 is where the two tables disagree most sharply, ' . $c3 . ' shifts for Camila against ' . $r3 . ' for Rosa. A boundary of more than 3 steps over that value, so the agreement further out is all the student sees; a boundary of 3 or more pulls it in and the difference appears at once.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><strong>Answer:</strong> part a is ' . $pctC . ' percent and part b is ' . $pctR . ' percent, the same share. For 3 or more minutes, Camila is ' . $pctC3 . ' percent and Rosa is ' . $pctR3 . ' percent. The shares for more than 3 agree because the boundary sits just above 3, the one value where the two tables differ most, so drawing it there hides the difference.</p>';

