// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A bank offers two savings accounts. Both pay a nominal annual interest rate of $rate%. You have $prin dollars to deposit, and you will leave it in the account for $yrs years, making no other deposits and no withdrawals.</p>

<p><b>Option A</b> compounds interest once a year, at the end of each year.</p>

<p><b>Option B</b> compounds interest every month, twelve times a year, at the same nominal annual rate of $rate%.</p>

<p>Part a: What is the balance in Option A after $yrs years? Round to the nearest cent.<br />
$answerbox[0]</p>

<p>Part b: What is the balance in Option B after $yrs years? Round to the nearest cent.<br />
$answerbox[1]</p>

<p>Part c: How much more interest does Option B earn than Option A over the $yrs years? Subtract your part a balance from your part b balance, and round to the nearest cent.<br />
$answerbox[2]</p>

<p>Part d: Which statement best explains why Option B earns more?<br />
$answerbox[3]</p>

<p>Enter amounts in dollars, numbers only, with no dollar signs and no commas.</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p>Step 1: Option A compounds once a year, so the annual rate of ' . $rate . ' percent becomes ' . $decAnn . ' as a decimal. The balance is ' . $prin . ' times 1 plus ' . $decAnn . ' raised to the power of ' . $yrs . ', which is ' . $ansA . ' dollars.</p><p>Step 2: Option B compounds twelve times a year, so each of the ' . $nPer . ' periods uses the decimal rate ' . $decMon . ', which is ' . $rate . ' percent divided by 12. The balance is ' . $prin . ' times 1 plus ' . $decMon . ' raised to the power of ' . $nPer . ', which is ' . $ansM . ' dollars.</p><p>Step 3: Option B earns ' . $ansM . ' minus ' . $ansA . ' = ' . $ansD . ' dollars more. Both accounts start with the same ' . $prin . ' dollars and neither has a withdrawal, so the difference in ending balances is exactly the difference in interest earned.</p><p>Step 4: Option B earns more because interest is credited twelve times a year instead of once. Each credit is added to the balance, so the next interest computation is done on a slightly larger amount. The nominal annual rate is the same for both accounts, so the gap grows with time but stays modest.</p><p>Answer: ' . $ansA . ' dollars in Option A, ' . $ansM . ' dollars in Option B, and ' . $ansD . ' dollars more interest from Option B.</p>';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Evaluating Logarithms Base 2, 3, or 4 - evaluate logs with base 2, 3, or 4 whose arguments are powers of the base, reciprocals of powers, odd powers of 2 with base 4, and radicals ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$anstypes = array("number","number","calculated","calculated");

$bases = diffrands(2,4,2);

$ba = $bases[0];
if ($ba==2) { $pa = rand(3,6); } else { $pa = rand(2,4); }
$argA = $ba**$pa;
$exprA = "log_".$ba."(".$argA.")";

$bb = $bases[1];
if ($bb==2) { $pb = rand(2,5); } else { $pb = rand(1,3); }
$argB = $bb**$pb;
$exprB = "log_".$bb."(1/".$argB.")";

$mc = 2*rand(0,2)+1;
$sc = rand(0,1);
if ($sc==0) { $kc = $mc; } else { $kc = -$mc; }
$powC = 2**$mc;
if ($kc>0) { $argC = $powC; } else { $argC = "1/".$powC; }
$exprC = "log_4(".$argC.")";

$bd = rand(2,3);
$nd = rand(2,3);
if ($nd==2) {
  $argD = "sqrt(".$bd.")";
  $rewD = "sqrt(".$bd.") = ".$bd."^(1/2)";
} else {
  $argD = "root(3)(".$bd.")";
  $rewD = "root(3)(".$bd.") = ".$bd."^(1/3)";
}
$exprD = "log_".$bd."(".$argD.")";

$answer[0] = $pa;
$answer[1] = -$pb;
$answer[2] = $kc/2;
$answer[3] = 1/$nd;

$answerformat[2] = "fracordec";
$answerformat[3] = "fracordec";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>Evaluate each logarithm. Give exact answers as integers or fractions.</p>
<p>Recall that `log_b(x) = y` means `b^y = x`.</p>
<p>a. `$exprA =` $answerbox[0]</p>
<p>b. `$exprB =` $answerbox[1]</p>
<p>c. `$exprC =` $answerbox[2]</p>
<p>d. `$exprD =` $answerbox[3]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$guideA = '<p><b>Part a.</b> Step 1: Ask what power of '.$ba.' gives '.$argA.'. Step 2: Write the argument as a power of the base: `'.$argA.' = '.$ba.'^'.$pa.'`. Step 3: A logarithm and a power with the same base undo each other, so `'.$exprA.' = '.$pa.'`. Answer: '.$pa.'</p>';
$guideB = '<p><b>Part b.</b> Step 1: Ask what power of '.$bb.' gives `1/'.$argB.'`. Step 2: Write the argument as a power of the base. First `'.$argB.' = '.$bb.'^'.$pb.'`, and a reciprocal means a negative exponent, so `1/'.$argB.' = '.$bb.'^(-'.$pb.')`. Step 3: The logarithm returns the exponent, so `'.$exprB.' = -'.$pb.'`. Answer: -'.$pb.'</p>';
$guideC = '<p><b>Part c.</b> Step 1: The base is 4, but the argument is a power of 2. Since `sqrt(4) = 2`, we have `2 = 4^(1/2)`. Step 2: Rewrite the argument as a power of 4: `'.$argC.' = 2^('.$kc.') = (4^(1/2))^('.$kc.') = 4^('.$kc.'/2)`. Step 3: The logarithm returns the exponent, so `'.$exprC.' = '.$kc.'/2`. Answer: `'.$kc.'/2`</p>';
$guideD = '<p><b>Part d.</b> Step 1: Rewrite the radical as a power of the base: `'.$rewD.'`. Step 2: The argument is now `'.$bd.'^(1/'.$nd.')`, so we are finding `log_'.$bd.'('.$bd.'^(1/'.$nd.'))`. Step 3: The logarithm returns the exponent, so the value is `1/'.$nd.'`. Answer: `1/'.$nd.'`</p>';
$solutionguide = $guideA . $guideB . $guideC . $guideD;

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Simplex Candle Shop: choose the leaving variable in the first pivot of a simplex tableau, then report the optimal pillar and taper counts, the maximum profit, and the molds left unused ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$a = rand(0,2);
$waxLimit = 22 + 3*$a;
$laborLimit = 20 + 3*$a;
$moldLimit = rand(9 + $a, 10 + $a);
$pillarsOpt = 8 + $a;
$tapersOpt = 6 + $a;
$maxProfit = 6*$pillarsOpt + 5*$tapersOpt;
$moldUnused = $moldLimit - $pillarsOpt;
$waxOz = 2*$waxLimit;
$laborMin = 5*$laborLimit;
$firstPivotP = 6*$moldLimit;
$waxRatio = round($waxLimit/2, 2);
$waxAfter = $waxLimit - 2*$moldLimit;
$laborAfter = round(($laborLimit - $moldLimit)/2, 2);
$waxUsed = 2*$pillarsOpt + $tapersOpt;
$laborUsed = $pillarsOpt + 2*$tapersOpt;

$anstypes = "choices,number,number,number,number";

$questions[0] = array("x, the number of pillar candles", "y, the number of taper candles", "s1, the slack for wax", "s2, the slack for labor", "s3, the slack for the molds");
$answer[0] = 4;
$answer[1] = $pillarsOpt;
$answer[2] = $tapersOpt;
$answer[3] = $maxProfit;
$answer[4] = $moldUnused;

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>The Simplex Candle Shop makes pillar candles and taper candles. Let `x` be the number of pillar candles and `y` the number of taper candles produced in one batch.</p>
<ul>
<li>Each pillar candle uses 4 ounces of wax and each taper candle uses 2 ounces. The shop has $waxOz ounces of wax on hand.</li>
<li>Each pillar candle needs 5 minutes of labor and each taper candle needs 10 minutes. The shop has $laborMin minutes of labor available.</li>
<li>Each pillar candle must sit in a mold while it cools, and the shop owns only $moldLimit molds, so `x <= $moldLimit`.</li>
<li>The profit is &#36;6 on each pillar candle and &#36;5 on each taper candle.</li>
</ul>
<p>The shop wants to maximize `P = 6x + 5y` subject to `2x + y <= $waxLimit` for wax, `x + 2y <= $laborLimit` for labor, `x <= $moldLimit` for molds, and `x >= 0, y >= 0`.</p>
<p>Adding the slacks `s_1`, `s_2`, `s_3` gives the initial simplex tableau below. The bottom row is the objective row.</p>
<table border='1' cellpadding='6' style='border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center'>
<tr><th>Basic</th><th>x</th><th>y</th><th>s<sub>1</sub></th><th>s<sub>2</sub></th><th>s<sub>3</sub></th><th>RHS</th></tr>
<tr><td>s<sub>1</sub></td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>$waxLimit</td></tr>
<tr><td>s<sub>2</sub></td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>$laborLimit</td></tr>
<tr><td>s<sub>3</sub></td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>$moldLimit</td></tr>
<tr><td>P</td><td>-6</td><td>-5</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Part a.</b> The entering variable is chosen by the most negative entry in the bottom row, and the leaving variable by the smallest ratio test. Which variable leaves the basis in the first pivot? $answerbox[0]</p>
///
<p><b>Part b.</b> How many pillar candles should the shop make in the profit-maximizing batch? $answerbox[1]</p>
///
<p><b>Part c.</b> How many taper candles should the shop make in the profit-maximizing batch? $answerbox[2]</p>
///
<p><b>Part d.</b> What is the maximum profit, in dollars? $answerbox[3]</p>
///
<p><b>Part e.</b> At the profit-maximizing batch, how many of the $moldLimit molds go unused? $answerbox[4]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = "<details><summary>Show solution</summary><p><b>Step 1 - the entering variable.</b> The bottom row of the initial tableau holds -6 under x and -5 under y. The most negative entry is -6, so x, the number of pillar candles, enters the basis.</p><p><b>Step 2 - the leaving variable.</b> Divide each positive entry of the x column into the right-hand side of its own row. The wax row gives {$waxRatio}, the labor row gives {$laborLimit}, and the mold row gives {$moldLimit}. The smallest of these ratios is {$moldLimit}, so s3, the slack for the molds, leaves the basis. That is the answer to part a.</p><p><b>Step 3 - pivot to the optimum.</b> The first pivot gives x = {$moldLimit}, y = 0, and P = {$firstPivotP}. The bottom row still carries -5 under y, so y enters next, and the ratio test in the y column compares {$waxAfter} with {$laborAfter}. The smaller is {$waxAfter}, so s1 leaves. One more pivot brings s3 back in and takes s2 out, and in that tableau no entry of the bottom row is negative, so the tableau is optimal: x = {$pillarsOpt} and y = {$tapersOpt}, with P = {$maxProfit}.</p><p><b>Step 4 - check the resources.</b> Wax used is {$waxUsed} ounces against {$waxOz} on hand, and labor used is {$laborUsed} minutes against {$laborMin} available, so both of those constraints are tight. Only {$pillarsOpt} of the {$moldLimit} molds are needed, so {$moldUnused} molds sit unused.</p><p><b>Answers.</b> Part a: s3, the slack for the molds. Part b: {$pillarsOpt} pillar candles. Part c: {$tapersOpt} taper candles. Part d: {$maxProfit} dollars. Part e: {$moldUnused} molds.</p></details>";

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Handshakes - counts the handshakes among n people with nC2, explains why order does not matter, then adds a late arrival to the group ===
