<p>Each description below explains how a sample was chosen from a population. For each description, select the sampling method that was used.</p>

<p>Each method in the list is used at most once, and some of the methods listed are not used at all.</p>

$answerbox

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide  = '<details><summary>Show solution</summary>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 1 - Ask two questions of every description.</b> Was the population split into groups before anyone was chosen, and what exactly was random about the final selection? The answers to those two questions name the method.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 2 - The whole list at once.</b> The study that numbers all ' . $popNum . ' ' . $unitWord . ' from 1 to ' . $popNum . ' and lets a random number generator pick ' . $srsSize . ' of the numbers splits the population into nothing and favours no position on the list. That is a <b>simple random sample</b>.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 3 - Two group-based methods, told apart by what is random.</b> The study that draws a random sample of ' . $strataPer . ' from each of the ' . $strataCount . ' groups keeps every group represented in the sample, so the groups are strata and the method is a <b>stratified sample</b>. The study that selects ' . $clusPick . ' of the ' . $clusCount . ' ' . $clusWord . ' at random and then surveys all of the ' . $unitWord . ' inside the chosen ones made the groups themselves the random part, so the method is a <b>cluster sample</b>. In the stratified study the randomness happens inside every group; in the cluster study it happens once, between groups.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 4 - The remaining two.</b> The study that chooses one of the first ' . $stepK . ' positions as a random start and then takes every ' . $stepK . 'th position after it randomizes only the start. Once the start is fixed the rest of the sample is forced, so it is a <b>systematic sample</b>. The study that surveys the first ' . $convSize . ' ' . $unitWord . ' who walk past ' . $placeWord . ' randomizes nothing at all and takes whoever is easiest to reach, so it is a <b>convenience sample</b>.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 5 - Rule out the methods that are not used.</b> A census would have surveyed all ' . $popNum . ' ' . $unitWord . ', with nobody left out, and a voluntary response sample would have let people put themselves into the sample. No description does either of those things.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Answer:</b> the five studies are a simple random sample, a stratified sample, a cluster sample, a systematic sample, and a convenience sample, one of each.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '</details>';

