// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: chebyshev-percent-within-k-sd-and-the-exact-share-claim - Chebyshev's Rule: give the guaranteed minimum percent within 2, 3 and 4.5 standard deviations and reject the claim that the rule gives an exact share of the data ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$nObs = rand(60,400);
$mu = rand(40,95);
$sd = rand(2,8);

$lo2 = $mu - 2*$sd;
$hi2 = $mu + 2*$sd;
$lo3 = $mu - 3*$sd;
$hi3 = $mu + 3*$sd;
$lo45 = $mu - 4.5*$sd;
$hi45 = $mu + 4.5*$sd;

$pct2 = round(100*(1 - 1/4), 4);
$pct3 = round(100*(1 - 1/9), 4);
$pct45 = round(100*(1 - 1/20.25), 4);

$anstypes = "number,number,number,choices";
$abstolerance = array(0.05,0.05,0.05,0.05);

$answer[0] = $pct2;
$answer[1] = $pct3;
$answer[2] = $pct45;
$answer[3] = 0;

$questions[3] = array(
  "No. The rule gives a minimum, not an exact share, so the true percent within 2 standard deviations could be anything from 75% to 100%.",
  "Yes. The rule is an equation, so exactly 75% of the observations lie within 2 standard deviations of the mean.",
  "No. The rule applies only to data that is bell shaped, so it says nothing at all about this data set.",
  "No. The rule gives a maximum, so at most 75% of the observations lie within 2 standard deviations of the mean."
);

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A data set records the value of one measurement for each of $nObs subjects. The mean of the data set is `$mu` and the standard deviation is `$sd`. Nothing is known about the shape of the distribution: it could be skewed, it could contain outliers, and it need not be bell shaped.</p>

<p>Chebyshev's Rule says that for <em>any</em> data set, at least `1 - 1/k^2` of the observations lie within `k` standard deviations of the mean.</p>

<p>For parts a, b and c, enter the percent rounded to two decimal places, using the number only and no percent sign.</p>

<p><strong>Part a.</strong> At least what percent of the observations lie within 2 standard deviations of the mean? $answerbox[0] %</p>

<p><strong>Part b.</strong> At least what percent of the observations lie within 3 standard deviations of the mean? $answerbox[1] %</p>

<p><strong>Part c.</strong> At least what percent of the observations lie within 4.5 standard deviations of the mean? $answerbox[2] %</p>

<p><strong>Part d.</strong> A classmate says: "Chebyshev's Rule shows that exactly 75% of these observations lie within 2 standard deviations of the mean." Which response is correct? $answerbox[3]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1: State the rule.</strong> Chebyshev\'s Rule says that for any data set, whatever the shape, at least `1 - 1/k^2` of the observations lie within `k` standard deviations of the mean. The rule hands back a guaranteed minimum, never the actual share in a particular data set.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Use k = 2.</strong> `1 - 1/2^2 = 1 - 1/4 = 0.75`, so at least 75% of the observations lie between `$lo2` and `$hi2`, that is within 2 standard deviations of the mean `$mu`.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Use k = 3.</strong> `1 - 1/3^2 = 1 - 1/9 = 0.8889`, so at least 88.89% of the observations lie between `$lo3` and `$hi3`.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Use k = 4.5.</strong> `1 - 1/4.5^2 = 1 - 1/20.25 = 0.9506`, so at least 95.06% of the observations lie between `$lo45` and `$hi45`.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5: Answer part d.</strong> The numbers 75%, 88.89% and 95.06% are floors. The real percent of observations falling inside each interval may be larger, anywhere up to 100%, and the rule cannot say which it is. A classmate who reads exactly 75% has turned a minimum into an exact share, which is not what the rule states. The response that the rule describes only bell shaped data is wrong for the same reason the rule is useful here: Chebyshev\'s Rule holds for every distribution, of any shape.</p>

<p><strong>Answer:</strong> part a 75%, part b 88.89%, part c 95.06%, part d the statement that the rule gives a minimum rather than an exact share, so the true percent could be anywhere from 75% up to 100%.</p>';

