$answersize[2] = "3,1";

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>Let `A = $Amat` and `B = $Bmat`.</p>
<p>You will solve the matrix equation `A X = B` with the inverse method, `X = A^(-1) B`. Every entry of `A^(-1)` and of `X` is an integer.</p>
<p><b>a.</b> Find the determinant of `A`. It is nonzero, so `A^(-1)` exists.</p>
<p>`det(A) =` $answerbox[0]</p>
<p><b>b.</b> Find the inverse matrix `A^(-1)`.</p>
<p>$answerbox[1]</p>
<p><b>c.</b> Use `A^(-1)` to solve `A X = B` for the matrix `X`.</p>
<p>$answerbox[2]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1 &mdash;</b> Find the determinant. Expanding along the first row with the cofactors found in Step 2, `det(A) = (' . $a11 . ')(' . $c11 . ') + (' . $a12 . ')(' . $c12 . ') + (' . $a13 . ')(' . $c13 . ') = ' . $detA . '`. Since the determinant is not zero, `A^(-1)` exists.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 2 &mdash;</b> Find the inverse. Each cofactor is a signed 2 by 2 determinant, giving the cofactor matrix `C = ' . $Cmat . '`. The inverse is the transpose of `C` divided by the determinant `' . $detA . '`, so `A^(-1) = ' . $Imat . '`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 3 &mdash;</b> Multiply both sides of `A X = B` on the left by `A^(-1)` to get `X = A^(-1) B`. Then `X = ' . $Imat . ' ' . $Bmat . ' = ' . $Xmat . '`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Answer:</b> `det(A) = ' . $detA . '`, `A^(-1) = ' . $Imat . '`, and `X = ' . $Xmat . '`.</p>';

// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: sum-greater-than-a-cutoff - Given a population mean, standard deviation and sample size, compute the mean and SD of the sum of n independent observations and the normal probability that the sum exceeds a cutoff ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$pairIdx = rand(0,5);
$muList    = array(12,16,20,25,40,50);
$sdList    = array(2,4,6,6,8,10);
$mu    = $muList[$pairIdx];
$sigma = $sdList[$pairIdx];

$nIdx    = rand(0,5);
$nList   = array(9,16,25,36,49,64);
$rootList = array(3,4,5,6,7,8);
$n     = $nList[$nIdx];
$rootN = $rootList[$nIdx];

$meanSum = $n * $mu;
$sdSum   = $sigma * $rootN;
$halfSd  = $sdSum / 2;

$zNumList = array(-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4);
$phiList  = array(0.0228,0.0668,0.1587,0.3085,0.6915,0.8413,0.9332,0.9772);
$j    = rand(0,7);
$zNum = $zNumList[$j];
$z    = $zNum / 2;
$phi  = $phiList[$j];

$cutoff = $meanSum + $zNum * $halfSd;

$probAbove = round(1 - $phi, 4);

$anstypes = "number,number,number";
$answer = array($meanSum, $sdSum, $probAbove);
$abstolerance = 0.001;

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A spice company uses a machine to fill pouches. The net weight of a single pouch is approximately normally distributed with mean `$mu` grams and standard deviation `$sigma` grams. The pouches are filled independently of one another.</p>

<p>A retail display box holds `$n` pouches. Let `S` be the total net weight of the `$n` pouches in one box.</p>

<p><strong>a.</strong> Find the mean of `S`, in grams.<br />
$answerbox[0] grams</p>

<p><strong>b.</strong> Find the standard deviation of `S`, in grams.<br />
$answerbox[1] grams</p>

<p><strong>c.</strong> Find the probability that the total net weight of the pouches in one box is greater than `$cutoff` grams. Round your answer to 4 decimal places.<br />
`P(S > $cutoff) =` $answerbox[2]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><strong>Step 1 &mdash; Mean of the sum.</strong> For independent observations the means add, so the mean of the total is `mu_S = n mu = ' . $n . ' times ' . $mu . ' = ' . $meanSum . '` grams. Dividing by nothing else is needed: the box simply holds ' . $n . ' pouches, each contributing ' . $mu . ' grams on average.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2 &mdash; Standard deviation of the sum.</strong> Variances add, not standard deviations. The variance of the total is `n sigma^2`, so the standard deviation of the total is `sigma_S = sqrt(n) sigma = ' . $rootN . ' times ' . $sigma . ' = ' . $sdSum . '` grams. Notice that this is larger than the standard deviation of a single pouch but smaller than ' . $n . ' times it.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3 &mdash; Standardize and read the normal table.</strong> A sum of independent normal observations is normal, so `z = (S - mu_S)/sigma_S` is standard normal. For the cutoff ' . $cutoff . ' grams, `z = (' . $cutoff . ' - ' . $meanSum . ')/' . $sdSum . ' = ' . $z . '`. The probability of exceeding the cutoff is the area to the right of this z-score: `1 - ' . $phi . ' = ' . $probAbove . '`.</p>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> mean `' . $meanSum . '` grams, standard deviation `' . $sdSum . '` grams, and `P(S > ' . $cutoff . ') = ' . $probAbove . '`.</p>';

