// === NAME - DESCRIPTION: Logarithmic Properties-09: Power Property - Apply log_b(x^n) = n log_b x to evaluate logarithmic expressions with positive and negative exponents and to identify an equivalent expression ===
// === SET QUESTION TYPE TO: multipart ===

// === COMMON CONTROL ===
$logX = rand(2,5);
$powA = rand(3,5);
$powB = rand(2,4);
$powC = rand(6,9);

$anstypes = "number,number,choices";

$answer[0] = $powA*$logX;
$answer[1] = -$powB*$logX;
$answer[2] = 0;

$questions[2] = array("`$powC log_b x`", "`log_b x + $powC`", "`log_b x - $powC`", "`x log_b $powC`", "`log_b x / $powC`");

// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>Recall the <b>power property</b> of logarithms: for any base `b` with `b > 0` and `b != 1`, and any real number `n`,</p>
<p style="text-align:center">`log_b(x^n) = n log_b x`</p>
<p>Suppose we know that `log_b x = $logX` for some fixed base `b`.</p>
<p><b>Part a.</b> Use the power property to find `log_b(x^$powA)`. Enter a number. $answerbox[0]</p>
<p><b>Part b.</b> Write `1/x^$powB` as a power of `x`, then use the power property to find `log_b(1/x^$powB)`. Enter a number. $answerbox[1]</p>
<p><b>Part c.</b> Which of these is equal to `log_b(x^$powC)`? $answerbox[2]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1. State the power property.</b> For any base `b` with `b > 0` and `b != 1`, and any real number `n`, `log_b(x^n) = n log_b x`. The exponent comes out front as a multiplier. The base stays the base and the argument stays the argument.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 2. Part a.</b> We are given `log_b x = ' . $logX . '`. The power property gives `log_b(x^' . $powA . ') = ' . $powA . ' log_b x = ' . $powA . ' * ' . $logX . ' = ' . ($powA*$logX) . '`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 3. Part b.</b> First write the reciprocal as a negative power: `1/x^' . $powB . ' = x^(-' . $powB . ')`. Then `log_b(1/x^' . $powB . ') = log_b(x^(-' . $powB . ')) = -' . $powB . ' log_b x = -' . $powB . ' * ' . $logX . ' = ' . (-$powB*$logX) . '`.</p>';
$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 4. Part c.</b> Apply the power property once: `log_b(x^' . $powC . ') = ' . $powC . ' log_b x`. The exponent becomes a coefficient on the outside. It is not added to the logarithm, it is not subtracted from it, it does not become the new base, and the logarithm is not divided by it.</p>';

