// === QUESTION TEXT ===
<p>A community garden club has $n members. A committee of $k members is to be chosen from them. One member, $who, has volunteered to serve if asked.</p>

<p><b>Part a.</b> In how many ways can the committee be chosen if there are no restrictions? $answerbox[0]</p>
///
<p><b>Part b.</b> In how many ways can the committee be chosen if $who must be on it? $answerbox[1]</p>
///
<p><b>Part c.</b> In how many ways can the committee be chosen if $who must not be on it? $answerbox[2]</p>
///
<p><b>Part d.</b> Which statement correctly describes how the count in part b is obtained?</p>
<p>$answerbox[3]</p>

// === ANSWER ===
$solutionguide = '<p><b>Step 1, part a.</b> With no restriction the club chooses ' . $k . ' of its ' . $n . ' members, and the order in which they are named does not matter, so the count is the combination `C(' . $n . ', ' . $k . ')` which equals ' . $total . '.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 2, part b.</b> If ' . $who . ' must serve, that seat is settled before anything else is decided. The committee still needs ' . $km1 . ' more people, and they must come from the ' . $nm1 . ' members other than ' . $who . ', so the count is `C(' . $nm1 . ', ' . $km1 . ')` which equals ' . $withMember . '.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 3, part c.</b> Leaving ' . $who . ' off is the same as filling all ' . $k . ' seats from the other ' . $nm1 . ' members, so the count is `C(' . $nm1 . ', ' . $k . ')` which equals ' . $withoutMember . '. The subtraction agrees, because every committee either includes ' . $who . ' or does not: ' . $total . ' minus ' . $withMember . ' equals ' . $withoutMember . '.</p>';

$solutionguide .= '<p><b>Step 4, part d.</b> The count in part b is built by seating ' . $who . ' first and then filling the remaining ' . $km1 . ' seats from the other ' . $nm1 . ' members. The other statements do not describe that count: choosing ' . $k . ' people from the ' . $nm1 . ' others counts the committees that leave ' . $who . ' out, choosing ' . $k . ' more after seating ' . $who . ' tries to fill one seat too many, and dividing by 2 throws away a factor that was never double counted.</p>';

