$solutionguide = '<p>Step 1 - Sort the descriptions into two families. Some talk about a whole group of individuals, and some talk about a single number.</p>
<p>Step 2 - Take the whole-group descriptions first. The entire group we want information about is the population. The part of the population we actually collect data from is the sample. A study that attempts to reach every individual in the population is a census.</p>
<p>Step 3 - Take the single-number descriptions. A number that describes a characteristic of the whole population is a parameter. A number computed from the data in a sample is a statistic. What separates them is where the number comes from, not how it is calculated.</p>
<p>Answer: Population = the entire group of individuals about which we want information. Sample = the part of the population from which we actually collect data. Parameter = a number that describes a characteristic of the whole population. Statistic = a number computed from the data in a sample. Census = a study that attempts to collect data from every individual in the population.</p>
<p>The unused description is either the list from which the sample is drawn, which is the sampling frame, or a count of the individuals in the sample, which is the sample size. Neither of those names a population, a sample, a parameter, a statistic, or a census.</p>';

